    "predecessor": "Vikrama",
    "successor": "Maravarman Sundara I",
    "common_titles": null,
    "meykirti_prashasti": "poovin kizhathi",
    "short_bio": "Jatavarman Kulasekhara I served as a Pandyan monarch who ruled during a period of rising regional power and military campaigns. His reign helped consolidate Pandyan authority in southern India prior to the major imperial expansions led by his successors.",
    "long_bio": "He ruled before the period of Pandyan revival. He succeeded his father Vikrama Pandya of the civil war.\nDuring his reign, he withheld tribute from the Chola empire while Kulothunga Chola III fought the Hoysalas. This rebellion provoked a Chola invasion in 1205. The invaders sacked Madurai and destroyed the ancient coronation hall. Kulasekhara fled before surrendering with his family. Kulothunga restored the crown under Chola suzerainty. This defeat set the stage for later Pandyan retaliation under his successor Maravarman Sundara Pandya I.",
    "notes": null,
    "website_slug": "jatavarman-kulasekara-i",
    "sort_order": 41.0,
    "image": null
  },
  {
    "king_id": "pandya_maravarman_sundara_i",
    "king_name_standard": "Maravarman Sundara Pandya I",
    "ruling_status": "primary",
    "dynasty": "Pandya",
    "dynasty_branch": "Imperial",
    "accession_year_ce": 1216.0,
    "end_year_ce": 1238.0,
    "century": "13th century",
    "predecessor": "Jatavarman Kulasekara I",
    "successor": "Jatavarman Kulasekara II",
    "common_titles": "sonadukondan, sonaduvazhangiaruliya, kaliyugaraman, atisayapandyadevan",
    "meykirti_prashasti": null,
    "short_bio": "Founder of the second Pandya empire. He invaded the Chola country, sacked Thanjavur and Uraiyur, and forced the submission of Kulottunga III, beginning the period of Pandya ascendancy.",
    "long_bio": "He reversed centuries of Chola dominance and founded the second Pandyan empire, establishing the Pandyas as the paramount power in South India. \nTo avenge the prior destruction of Madurai, he invaded the Chola heartland, sacked Thanjavur and Uraiyur, and drove the Chola monarchs into exile. He performed a virabhisheka at the Chola coronation hall in Ayirattali and conducted a thulabaram at the Chidambaram temple. After Hoysala intervention threatened his forces, he restored the Chola crown only after extracting formal submission at Pon Amaravathi. He subsequently repelled Oriya incursions at Srirangam and allied with the Kadava chieftain Kopperunchinga I against Chola and Hoysala influence, laying the foundation for enduring Pandyan ascendancy.",
    "notes": "Key figure in the Pandya revival.",
    "website_slug": "maravarman-sundara-i",
    "sort_order": 42.0,
    "image": null
  },
